# ============================ SYSADMIN 3 ============================
LESSONS_S3 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Masters: the registers everything links to", "11", "<p>This module covers master data governance: what masters are and why they concentrate risk, naming, duplicate control and the merge tool, creation governance, the Data Import tool, field hygiene, the monthly register reads, and the HealthTrade cleanup.</p><p><b>What a master is.</b> Module 1 split the doctypes into transactions (docstatus, posted, immutable) and masters (current-state records, edited in place): <b>Customer, Supplier, Item, Warehouse, Employee, Item Group, Territory, UOM, Price List</b> — the registers. Every transaction links into them through Link fields: an invoice is mostly pointers — this Customer, these Items, this Warehouse — plus quantities and dates.</p><p><b>Why masters concentrate risk.</b> The Link structure means a master error multiplies by its usage:</p><p>• A transaction error is <b>one</b> wrong document, corrected by one correction.<br>• A master error is wrong in <b>every document that links to it, forever, until fixed</b>: the Item with the wrong default income account mis-posts every sale; the Customer created twice splits one relationship's history across two records — two partial statements, two partial agings, neither true; the Supplier with unverified bank details is the fraud channel module 3-of-Accounts guards.</p><p>This is why masters get their own governance module while transactions get form validations: the leverage is different.</p><p><b>The four properties of a governed register.</b> Everything in this module serves one of these:</p><p>1. <b>Unique</b> — one real-world entity, one record. Chapter 4's duplicate control.<br>2. <b>Named</b> — records identified by a deliberate scheme that reads well for years. Chapters 2-3.<br>3. <b>Complete</b> — the fields the machinery depends on (defaults, groups, tax categories) filled at creation, not discovered empty at posting time. Chapter 6.<br>4. <b>Owned</b> — each register has a named owner who approves creation and answers for hygiene. Chapter 5.</p><p><b>The sysadmin's position.</b> As with permissions: the sysadmin owns the <b>machinery</b> — naming series configured, create rights narrowed, the import tool governed, the merge performed, the hygiene reads scheduled — while the register <b>content</b> belongs to its owner: the item book to stock/procurement, the customer book to sales, the supplier book to procurement, the employee register to HR.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: naming — series and field-based. Chapter 3: the Naming Series tool. Chapter 4: duplicates and the Rename/Merge tool. Chapter 5: creation governance. Chapter 6: field hygiene and completeness. Chapter 7: the Data Import tool. Chapter 8: the monthly reads and the HealthTrade case. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Naming: series or field, decided per register", "12", "<p>Every document's name is its permanent identity — printed on statements, quoted in disputes, filed with returns. Each master doctype names its records by one of two schemes, and the choice is made deliberately per register, at birth.</p><p><b>Field-based naming.</b> The record is named by one of its own fields. <b>Customer Naming By</b> in Selling Settings and <b>Supplier Naming By</b> in Buying Settings offer exactly this choice: name customers by <b>Customer Name</b> (the record's ID is the trading name itself) or by <b>Naming Series</b> (CUST-00042 with the name as a field).</p><p>• Field-based reads beautifully — lists, links, and reports show the actual name.<br>• Its cost is collision and drift: two \"Emeka Stores\" cannot both exist under name-based IDs, and a legal rename leaves the ID carrying the old name (the Rename tool fixes the display, Chapter 4, but references show history).</p><p><b>Series naming.</b> The record is named by a counter pattern — CUST-.####, ITEM-.####. Neutral, collision-proof, rename-proof; the human-readable name lives in its field and can change freely. Its cost: IDs mean nothing without the record open.</p><p><b>The working defaults, per register.</b></p><p>• <b>Items:</b> the <b>Item Code</b> is the identity, and it deserves a designed scheme — module owners decide between structured codes (category prefixes) and plain sequences, but the sysadmin enforces one rule either way: <b>codes are assigned by the scheme, never invented at the keyboard.</b> The stock track's barcode and search behaviour all hang off this field.<br>• <b>Customers and Suppliers:</b> smaller books read best name-based; large or fast-growing books, or any book expecting name collisions (common personal trading names), run series-based with the name as a field. Decide once, record the reason.<br>• <b>Transactions:</b> always series — SINV-, PINV-, PE- — with the year segment where the business wants year-readable numbers (SINV-2026-). Module 8-of-Accounts' rule repeats here because the sysadmin implements it: <b>series are never restarted and never reused</b>; the invoice numbers on a filed VAT return must still make sense to an auditor in year seven.</p><p><b>The decision record.</b> One page in the implementation file: per doctype, the naming scheme, the reason, and the owner who agreed it. Naming disputes and \"why is this called that\" questions are answered by pointing — the same page-answers-it pattern as every policy in this programme.</p><p>Practical rule: naming changes after go-live are governed events of the heaviest kind, because names are woven through history. Get the decision right at birth; Chapter 3 sets the machinery.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Naming Series tool", "12", "<p>Open <b>Naming Series</b> (awesome bar). This tool defines the series patterns per doctype and manages their counters. Small screen, permanent consequences.</p><p><b>Setting the series.</b> Select the transaction type; the tool shows its current series options and lets you set the list — one or several patterns, one marked default. The pattern grammar:</p><p>• Fixed prefix text: <b>SINV-</b><br>• Year and month segments: <b>SINV-.YYYY.-</b> gives SINV-2026-00042; month segments where volume wants monthly readability.<br>• The counter: <b>.####</b> — four digits rolls at ten thousand; size the digits to a decade of volume, not a year.</p><p><b>Multiple series on one doctype — the lawful uses.</b> Several patterns on one doctype serve real needs: per-company series on a multi-company site (each entity's invoices numbered in its own stream — the legal-separation rule expressed in numbering), or per-branch series where the business wants branch-readable documents. The unlawful use is decorative proliferation: every added series is another counter to govern and another choice a user can get wrong at entry — where a series choice exists on the form, the <b>default per user or company must be wired</b> so the front line never chooses.</p><p><b>The current-value control.</b> The tool can read and set each series' current counter. Two lawful uses, one forbidden:</p><p>• <b>Lawful:</b> setting the opening counter at go-live so the new system continues the old numbering (the business's invoice history reads as one stream across the migration); and aligning a counter after a governed correction, documented.<br>• <b>Forbidden:</b> resetting a counter to reuse numbers. A reused invoice number is two different documents answering one identity — a filing-integrity failure the year-seven auditor finds.</p><p><b>Amended documents.</b> The framework names amendments as successors (the amended document carries its lineage) — the sysadmin's job is not to fight this: attempts to make amended documents reuse the original number break the audit chain the docstatus design exists to keep.</p><p><b>The series register.</b> The one-page record from Chapter 2 gains its machinery column: per doctype — the pattern, the default, the counter's opening value and date, and any per-company/per-branch mapping. Reviewed at the annual walk; every change between walks is a logged event with a reason.</p><p>Practical rule: test a new series by creating and cancelling one document in the test environment, and read the produced name aloud against the register. Ten seconds; it catches the misplaced dot, the missing year segment, and the wrong default before a month of documents inherits them.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Duplicates and the Rename/Merge tool", "13", "<p>Duplicates are the register disease: one real entity, two records, history split. This chapter is prevention, detection, and the cure.</p><p><b>How duplicates are born.</b> Three standard births: the search that failed (\"Emeka Stores\" exists as \"EMEKA STORES VENTURES\"; the creator searched, missed, created), the import that appended instead of updating (Chapter 7's first governance rule), and the open-creation site where every user can create and nobody checks. Prevention maps to each: search-first taught as the creation habit, import mode discipline, and Chapter 5's narrowed create rights.</p><p><b>What a duplicate costs while it lives.</b> The split history: two partial customer statements neither of which supports the collections call; two supplier records with two balances hiding one exposure; two item codes carrying one product's stock in two ledgers — the stock track's count variances born at the keyboard. Every month a duplicate lives, the split deepens.</p><p><b>Detection — the monthly duplicate read.</b> No single button finds them; the read is a procedure:</p><p>1. The register's list sorted by name, scanned for near-neighbours — the ten-minute eyeball that catches most.<br>2. The report view grouped where grouping exposes: customers by mobile number or tax ID (two records, one phone, one TIN — a duplicate confessing), items by barcode.<br>3. Candidates logged to the register owner for verdict: same entity, or genuinely two.</p><p><b>The cure — Rename with Merge.</b> Open the surviving record, choose <b>Rename</b> from the menu, enter the duplicate's name as the target... the direction matters, so state it as the procedure:</p><p>1. Decide the <b>survivor</b> (usually the older, fuller record) with the register owner.<br>2. On the <b>duplicate</b>, use Rename → enter the survivor's name → tick <b>Merge with existing</b>. The framework re-points the duplicate's entire linked history — invoices, payments, stock entries — onto the survivor and retires the duplicate.<br>3. Merging is heavier for some registers: <b>Items</b> merge only when their properties agree (stock UOM and similar structural fields), and merging warehouses or accounts is a planned event, not a hygiene click.<br>4. Log the merge: the two names, the survivor, the date, the owner's approval. The Rename tool's power is exactly why its use is a recorded event — and why <b>rename/merge rights stay with the sysadmin</b>, not the register's daily users.</p><p>After the merge, the reunited record tells the whole story: one statement, one aging, one stock history — which is the payoff to show the register owner the first time, because it converts search-first from a rule into a conviction.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Creation governance: who may create masters", "12", "<p>Chapter 4's third duplicate birth — open creation — is fixed structurally: master creation is a governed act, narrower than master use.</p><p><b>The permission design (module 2's machinery applied).</b> In the Role Permission Manager, split <b>Create from Read/Use</b> on each master doctype:</p><p>• Everyone whose job uses customers holds Read on Customer; the roles that may <b>create</b> customers are one or two — the register owner's role and, where volume demands, a named deputy.<br>• Items narrower still: the item book is the highest-leverage register (defaults, valuation, tax categories all hang on it), so Item create/write typically sits with stock or procurement ownership only.<br>• Employees: HR only. Suppliers: procurement only — with the bank-detail custody rules from the Accounts track riding on the same record.</p><p><b>The request flow.</b> Narrow creation needs a fast lane or the front line routes around it:</p><p>1. The requester (a salesperson meeting a new customer) submits the details through the agreed channel — a request doctype where the site has one, or the owner's standing form.<br>2. The owner (or deputy) searches first, then creates governed: named by the scheme, fields complete per Chapter 6, and linked back to the requester the same day.<br>3. The service standard is written — same-day for customers, agreed lead time for items — because a slow lane is how \"just this once\" exceptions are born.</p><p><b>Walk-in reality at the till.</b> Retail cannot wait for a request flow, and the design answers it without opening the register: POS sales run against the standing walk-in customer, and a <b>real</b> customer record is created only when a real relationship starts (credit, statements, history) — through the flow. The stock track's companion rule mirrors it: no selling against items that do not exist; emergency item creation is the owner's phone call, not the cashier's keyboard.</p><p><b>New-master verification, by register.</b> Creation governance carries the verification duties this programme has already placed:</p><p>• <b>Suppliers:</b> bank details entered from documents received through a known channel, and every later change treated as the governed master-data event the Accounts track specifies.<br>• <b>Customers taking credit:</b> the credit fields (limit, terms) set by the owner of credit policy, not defaulted.<br>• <b>Items:</b> the completeness checklist of Chapter 6 before first transaction.</p><p>Practical rule: measure the flow, not the rule. The monthly read (Chapter 8) counts masters created, by whom, and duplicates found — a rising duplicate count with narrowed rights means the request lane is too slow, and the fix is the lane.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Field hygiene: complete at creation", "12", "<p>A master that exists but is incomplete fails at posting time — the invoice that cannot find an income account, the item that will not stock-post, the customer invisible to their territory's reports. Completeness is enforced at creation, register by register.</p><p><b>The item book — the completeness checklist.</b> The Item is the most field-dependent master; an item is complete when:</p><p>• <b>Identity:</b> Item Code by the scheme, Item Name, <b>Item Group</b> (the reporting tree — an ungrouped item vanishes from every grouped read), stock UOM chosen deliberately (UOM changes after stock exists are surgery).<br>• <b>Behaviour flags:</b> Maintain Stock for stocked goods; Is Fixed Asset routing capital items to the Asset machinery; batch/serial flags where the stock design says so.<br>• <b>Defaults per company:</b> the Item Defaults table — income and expense accounts, default warehouses, buying/selling cost centres — the fields that make every future document post right without anyone thinking.<br>• <b>Tax:</b> the Item Tax Template where the item's rate differs from standard (the mixed-supply rule from the tax module, enforced at this keyboard).<br>• <b>Commerce:</b> the selling price on its Price List (Item Price record), barcodes where scanning runs.</p><p><b>Parties.</b> A customer is complete with: group and <b>Territory</b> (the analytical dimensions sales reads by), contact and address records (statements need somewhere to go — Process Statement Of Accounts mails these), payment terms, and the credit fields where credit is granted. A supplier adds: the bank details under custody rules, the <b>Tax Withholding Category</b> where WHT applies (the tax module's agent side starts at this field), and payment terms.</p><p><b>Enforcement — machinery over memos.</b></p><p>1. <b>Mandatory fields:</b> the high-dependency fields are made mandatory on the form (a governed customisation, module 5), so incomplete cannot save. Item Group and stock UOM are the classic candidates.<br>2. <b>Templates and defaults:</b> new-item templates per category pre-fill the defaults table; group-level defaults catch what item-level misses.<br>3. <b>The completeness read:</b> saved filters per register for the gaps that matter — items without Item Group, items without defaults for the active company, customers without territory or contact, suppliers without withholding category where the business withholds. Run monthly (Chapter 8); each hit routes to the register owner.</p><p>Practical rule: every field on this chapter's lists exists because some module's track depends on it — when a register owner asks why a field is mandatory, the answer names the downstream machinery: <i>this is what breaks when it is empty.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The Data Import tool", "13", "<p>Open <b>Data Import</b> (awesome bar). Bulk creation and bulk update run through this tool, and it is governed like the power it is: an import is a hundred keyboards typing at once.</p><p><b>The tool's anatomy.</b> A Data Import document names the target doctype and the <b>Import Type</b> — the single most consequential field on the screen:</p><p>• <b>Insert New Records</b> — creates. Used for genuine first loads and genuinely new populations.<br>• <b>Update Existing Records</b> — modifies matched records via their ID column. Used for corrections and enrichment.</p><p>Choosing Insert when the records exist is Chapter 4's duplicate factory at industrial scale; choosing Update with a wrong ID column writes one record's data onto another. The type is confirmed against the job before every run, out loud.</p><p><b>The template.</b> The tool's <b>Download Template</b> produces the doctype's own spreadsheet: correct columns, mandatory fields marked, one row per record (child tables as their own columns/sheets per the template's layout). Work in the downloaded template, never a hand-built sheet — half of import pain is column-name archaeology the template makes unnecessary.</p><p><b>The governed import, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. <b>Source and owner:</b> the data's origin named, the register owner's approval on record. An import without an owner is someone bulk-editing a register they do not answer for.<br>2. <b>Prepare in the template:</b> IDs present for updates; naming per the scheme for inserts; the completeness fields of Chapter 6 filled — an import that creates incomplete masters creates a hundred failures-at-posting-time.<br>3. <b>Dry-run a slice:</b> ten rows into the <b>test environment</b> first (module 5's test discipline applies to data as much as code). Read the created records against expectations.<br>4. <b>Back up first:</b> the on-demand backup before any large import (module 4's before-change rule). The import that goes wrong at row 3,000 is recovered by restore or by reversal; both start from the backup and the import's own log.<br>5. <b>Run, then read the result:</b> the tool reports successes and error rows with reasons. Error rows are fixed and re-run — not abandoned (a half-imported register is two half-truths).<br>6. <b>Log the event:</b> what, from where, how many, by whom, approved by whom — into the incident/ops book. The Data Import list itself is the technical trail; the log adds the governance.</p><p><b>Import rights.</b> Module 2's matrix flagged Import as a real permission: it stays with the sysadmin and the register owners who have run the procedure — never a general grant. One unauthorised \"helpful\" import can undo a quarter of register hygiene.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — The monthly reads & the HealthTrade cleanup", "13", "<p><b>The monthly master hygiene read (fifteen minutes, from saved filters).</b></p><p>1. <b>Creation review:</b> masters created this month, by register — each list read for scheme-named, owner-approved, complete. Off-process creations route to their owners; a rising count with clean process is growth, with dirty process it is drift.<br>2. <b>The duplicate scan:</b> Chapter 4's procedure per register; candidates to owners; merges performed and logged.<br>3. <b>The completeness gaps:</b> Chapter 6's saved filters run; hits routed; the counts trended — gaps should shrink toward zero and stay there.<br>4. <b>The disabled review:</b> masters retired this month (the <b>Disabled</b> checkbox is the master world's leaver procedure — records with history are disabled, never deleted, exactly like users), and any transacting-against-disabled errors read for pattern.<br>5. <b>Import log reconciliation:</b> every Data Import document this month matched to a governance log line.</p><p><b>The HealthTrade cleanup.</b> The register inventory after module 2's access work finds the item book carrying the years of open creation: <b>3,940 items</b>, of which a scan-and-group read surfaces <b>212 duplicate candidates</b>; customers name-based with <b>17 near-neighbour pairs</b>; and no naming decision on record anywhere.</p><p><b>Week one — the decisions.</b> The naming page is written with the owners: items keep their structured code scheme (codes by the scheme, never keyboard-invented); customers move to series naming for all <b>new</b> records (the collision history decided it) with existing names left standing; transaction series confirmed with year segments and counters sized to the decade. The Naming Series tool is set accordingly and one test document per series is created-and-cancelled in the test site, names read aloud.</p><p><b>Weeks two to four — the merges.</b> The 212 item candidates go to the stock owner for verdict: <b>74 confirmed duplicates</b> merged via Rename → Merge with existing (batched, logged, stock histories reunited — two counts that had argued for months agree the week their item codes become one), 96 confirmed distinct (variants mis-suspected), 42 retired outright as dead entries via the Disabled checkbox. The 17 customer pairs yield 11 merges — and the first reunited statement settles a standing dispute: the customer's \"missing\" payments had lived on the twin.</p><p><b>Week five — the fences go up.</b> Create rights narrowed per Chapter 5 (item creation to two names; customer creation to the sales owner and deputy with the same-day lane; the walk-in rule confirmed at the tills). Mandatory fields land on Item Group and stock UOM. The completeness filters are built and the first run routes <b>118 ungrouped items</b> to the stock owner — cleared inside the month.</p><p><b>The steady state.</b> Three months on, the monthly read runs its fifteen minutes: single-digit creations per register, all on-process; duplicate candidates near zero; completeness filters empty; one import (a price-list update) logged with its before-backup. The MD's line for the file: <i>the registers used to be a swamp we drained yearly; now they are a garden somebody weeds monthly.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the third law", "11", "<p>The register layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the open register.</b> Symptom: everyone creates everything; duplicates monthly; naming by keyboard mood; nobody answerable for any register. Cause: create rights never split from use rights (Chapter 5). Fix: the Role Permission Manager split, owners named per register, the request lane with its service standard, and the walk-in rule at the tills.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the duplicate swamp.</b> Symptom: split statements, split agings, stock in twin item codes, collection calls made on half a history. Cause: search-first never taught, imports run in Insert against existing populations, detection never scheduled (Chapters 4, 7). Fix: the drain — the scan, the owner verdicts, the Rename/Merge batches, logged — then the monthly scan keeping the water low.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the incomplete master.</b> Symptom: failures at posting time — invoices that cannot find accounts, items that will not stock-post, statements with nowhere to go; each fixed \"quickly\" at the counter by whoever is blocked. Cause: completeness unenforced at creation (Chapter 6). Fix: mandatory fields on the high-dependency columns, templates pre-filling defaults, and the completeness filters run monthly with hits routed to owners.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the ungoverned import.</b> Symptom: a register transformed overnight; nobody knows what ran, from what source, on whose approval; error rows abandoned; the counter reads a half-truth. Cause: Import held broadly and run casually (Chapter 7). Fix: Import rights narrowed, the six-step procedure (owner, template, test slice, backup, run-and-read, log) as the only lane, and the monthly import-log reconciliation.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — deletion as tidying.</b> Symptom: masters with history deleted to \"clean up\"; broken links; reports whose totals reference records that no longer exist; the audit trail orphaned. Cause: the Disabled checkbox unknown or ignored. Fix: the rule taught as the user rule's twin — <b>history is never deleted; retirement is the Disabled checkbox</b> — and Delete kept off master doctypes for daily roles in the permission matrix.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — series drift.</b> Symptom: counters reset, numbers reused, parallel series nobody chose, an invoice number the filed return cannot match. Cause: the Naming Series tool touched casually (Chapter 3). Fix: the series register page, current-value changes as logged events with lawful reasons only, and the annual walk reconciling tool to page.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the four properties of a governed register and the master-error multiplication; choose field-based versus series naming per register and defend the Customer/Supplier Naming By decision; operate the Naming Series tool — patterns, counters, the two lawful current-value uses and the forbidden one; run the duplicate read and the Rename → Merge with existing procedure with its logging; design creation governance with the request lane and the walk-in rule; enforce completeness by mandatory fields, templates, and the saved-filter reads; run the six-step governed import in the right Import Type; and run the monthly hygiene read.</p><p><b>The third law: masters enter governed, once — named by the scheme, owned by name, merged never duplicated, disabled never deleted.</b> Module 4 covers the layer beneath everything: backups and continuity.</p>"),
]

