# ============================ SYSADMIN 4 ============================
LESSONS_S4 = [
L("Chapter 1 — A backup untested is a hope", "11", "<p>This module covers the survival layer: what a backup contains, the two custodies and their schedules, the Download Backups page, restores and the drill, the before-change backup, the continuity runbook beyond data, and the HealthTrade rehearsal.</p><p><b>The stakes, stated flat.</b> The site holds the books, the stock ledger, the customer relationships, the tax evidence — the business's memory. Hardware fails, mistakes at scale happen (the import gone wrong, the bulk edit misfired), and hostile events exist. Backups are the difference between an incident and an ending. Every other module of this track assumes this one works.</p><p><b>The law, stated first because everything serves it: a backup untested is a hope.</b> A backup file that exists proves storage was consumed; only a <b>restore</b> proves the business can come back. The industry's standing tragedy is the business that backed up faithfully for years and discovered at the fire that the files were empty, partial, or unrestorable. The restore drill (Chapter 5) is therefore not an advanced topic — it is the module's centre, and the monthly calendar line module 1 already placed.</p><p><b>The two custodies.</b> On the managed platform, backup runs in both custodies of module 1's map, and the sysadmin must know exactly what each holds:</p><p>• <b>The host's custody (CloudERP.One):</b> the scheduled platform backups — nightly database and file backups on the infrastructure, replicated off-site per the hosting agreement. The sysadmin does not operate these but must hold their terms <b>in writing</b>: frequency, retention, off-site arrangement, and the two recovery numbers of Chapter 2.<br>• <b>The site's custody (this certification):</b> the periodic <b>downloaded copy</b> kept outside the platform entirely (Chapter 3's procedure), the <b>before-change backup</b> preceding every risky operation (Chapter 6), and the <b>restore verification</b> that tests the whole chain (Chapter 5).</p><p>Two custodies because each covers the other's failure mode: the host's machinery covers the site's daily risks at professional grade; the site's own off-platform copy covers the un-thinkable — the platform relationship itself failing, the account dispute, the regional event. A business whose only backups live inside the platform they back up has one custody, not two.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: what a backup contains, and the two recovery numbers. Chapter 3: the Download Backups page and the off-platform copy. Chapter 4: restore paths on a managed platform. Chapter 5: the restore drill. Chapter 6: the before-change backup. Chapter 7: the continuity runbook. Chapter 8: the HealthTrade rehearsal. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — What a backup contains, and the two numbers", "12", "<p><b>The anatomy.</b> A complete site backup is <b>two things</b>, and the distinction is this chapter's most practical fact:</p><p>1. <b>The database</b> — every document, master, setting, permission, and log: the structured memory. This is the file most people mean by \"the backup\".<br>2. <b>The files</b> — the attachments: the invoice scans, the signed contracts, the voucher images, the product photos — stored as public and private file archives <b>separate from the database dump</b>.</p><p>A database-only backup restores a system whose every attachment link is broken — and since this programme attaches evidence to entries as standard practice (remittance returns, import approvals, count sheets), the files <b>are</b> part of the books. The completeness rule: <b>a backup is database plus files, always, in both custodies.</b> Chapter 8 shows HealthTrade learning this the cheap way.</p><p><b>What is not in the site backup.</b> The apps (code) and platform configuration live in the host's custody and update stream — after a restore, the host re-provisions the site on current platform code with the restored data. This is why version notes matter in the backup log: data from a much older app version restores with a migration step the host performs.</p><p><b>The two recovery numbers — held in writing.</b> The hosting agreement's continuity terms compress into two numbers the sysadmin must be able to quote:</p><p>• <b>RPO — recovery point objective:</b> how much recent work can be lost. Nightly backups mean an RPO of up to twenty-four hours: a failure at 5pm can lose the day. The business decides — as a policy, with the owner — whether that is acceptable for its trade; where it is not, the answer is raising backup frequency with the host, not hoping.<br>• <b>RTO — recovery time objective:</b> how long a restore takes end to end — from decision to a working site. The number shapes Chapter 7's runbook: an RTO of hours means the degraded-operation procedures must carry hours, not minutes.</p><p>Both numbers are confirmed with the host, written on the continuity page, and re-confirmed at the annual review — because the numbers people assume are always better than the numbers the agreement states, and the gap is discovered at the worst possible moment.</p><p>Practical rule: the monthly backup verification (Chapter 5) checks three completeness facts before anything else: the database file present, <b>both</b> file archives present, and the dates matching the schedule. Presence is the cheap check; the restore is the real one.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — The Download Backups page and the off-platform copy", "12", "<p>Open <b>Download Backups</b> (awesome bar). This page is the site's own window on its backups: the recent backup sets listed with timestamps, each set offering its <b>database</b> download and its <b>files</b> downloads, plus the on-demand trigger to generate a fresh backup now. Two of this module's procedures run from this page.</p><p><b>Procedure one — the daily presence check (module 1's calendar line).</b> Open the page; confirm yesterday's set exists with a plausible size and today's schedule ran. Thirty seconds. A missing or shrunken backup is raised the same day with the five-item report — this is the check that catches the silently-dead schedule <b>before</b> the month that needed it.</p><p><b>Procedure two — the off-platform copy (monthly, after the restore drill).</b></p><p>1. Trigger a fresh on-demand backup (or take the latest nightly set).<br>2. Download <b>all components</b> — the database file and both file archives. The database-only download habit is this module's classic quiet failure.<br>3. Store the set <b>outside the platform</b>: the business's own encrypted storage — a cloud drive under the business's account, plus, where the policy wants it, an offline copy. The point of this copy is surviving events that include the platform, so it cannot live on the platform.<br>4. Verify the copy: sizes match, the archive opens, the set is complete.<br>5. Log it: date, set timestamp, components, where stored, verified by whom.<br>6. Retention per the written policy — typically a rolling set of monthly copies plus the year-end copies kept long-term with the audit file. The retention line is written on the continuity page beside the recovery numbers, so the question of how far back the business can reach has one answer in one place.</p><p><b>Custody of the copies.</b> The downloaded backup <b>is the entire business in a file</b> — every customer, every price, every salary. It is protected like the crown jewels it is:</p><p>• Stored encrypted, in storage the <b>business</b> controls (not a personal drive that leaves when a person does — the leaver procedure cannot chase personal storage).<br>• Access limited to the sysadmin and backstop, listed on the continuity page.<br>• Old copies beyond retention are properly destroyed, not abandoned on forgotten drives.</p><p>Practical rule: the off-platform copy is scheduled <b>after</b> the monthly restore drill on the calendar, so what goes off-platform each month is a set whose sibling was just proven restorable — the copy inherits the drill's confidence.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Restore paths on a managed platform", "12", "<p>Restores on a managed platform run through defined paths, and the sysadmin's competence is knowing which path fits which event <b>before</b> the event.</p><p><b>Path one — the full restore (the host performs it).</b> The site is lost or corrupted beyond repair: the restore is a support request of the highest severity — the five-item report plus three restore-specific facts: <b>which backup set</b> (the timestamp, chosen deliberately: the last set <b>before</b> the damaging event, which is not always the latest set), <b>restore where</b> (over the live site, or to a parallel site for inspection first — the cautious default when time allows), and <b>the authorisation</b> (a full restore discards everything after the restore point; the decision is the owner's, on the record, because the restore's cost is the RPO made real: the work since the set is re-entered from the paper trail).</p><p><b>Path two — the test-site restore (the drill's path, Chapter 5).</b> The same mechanics pointed at the test environment: a backup set restored to the test site, where it can be opened and read without touching production. This path is exercised monthly, which means the people, the request format, and the timing are all rehearsed when path one is suddenly needed — the drill is a fire drill precisely because it uses the real stairs.</p><p><b>Path three — the surgical recovery (restore-to-read).</b> The most common real-world case is not a lost site but a damaged <b>slice</b>: the import that mangled a register, the bulk edit that overwrote a field, the cancellation cascade. A full restore would sacrifice everything since the set to fix one register — the wrong trade. The surgical path:</p><p>1. Restore the pre-event backup <b>to the test site</b> (path two's mechanics).<br>2. Read the damaged slice from the test site — the correct values, exported.<br>3. Repair production <b>forward</b>: a governed Update-type Data Import (module 3's procedure) or documented corrections, applying the correct values as a new, logged change.<br>4. Production's history stays intact — the damage and the repair both on the record, which is this programme's correction philosophy applied at scale.</p><p><b>Choosing the path — the three questions.</b> What is damaged (everything, or a slice)? When was the last good state (which set)? What would a full restore cost (everything since the set — is the slice worth it)? The answers route the event; the runbook (Chapter 7) has them pre-printed.</p><p>Practical rule: in any restore conversation, name the backup set by timestamp in writing before anything runs. \"Restore the backup\" has destroyed more data than it has recovered; \"restore the 03:12 set from the 14th, to the test site\" is a procedure.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — The restore drill", "12", "<p>The module's centre. The drill converts backup files into proven recoverability, monthly, on the calendar.</p><p><b>The monthly drill, as a procedure.</b></p><p>1. <b>Pick this month's set</b> — vary it: sometimes the latest nightly, sometimes the off-platform copy from storage (the copy must prove itself too, or it is decoration).<br>2. <b>Restore to the test site</b> — path two. On the managed platform this is the standing arrangement with the host or the self-service test-site mechanism, whichever the agreement provides; the drill also keeps that arrangement itself exercised.<br>3. <b>Open and read.</b> Presence is not the test — <b>readability</b> is: log in; open the Trial Balance and compare a known figure; open a recent Sales Invoice <b>and its attachment</b> (the files archive proving itself); run one report; check the restored set's document count against production's for the same date.<br>4. <b>Time it.</b> The end-to-end minutes are the site's real RTO evidence — trended, and compared against the number on the continuity page. A drifting drill time is a finding.<br>5. <b>Sign it.</b> One log line: date, set, path, checks passed, minutes, by whom. Twelve lines a year; the auditor's favourite page in this module.</p><p><b>What the drill catches — each one a real failure mode found cheap:</b> the empty or truncated dump (storage consumed, nothing inside); the missing files archive (the database-only habit); the schedule that died silently weeks ago; the off-platform copy that never verified; the restore arrangement that changed at the host's end without the runbook noticing; and the RTO drifting from the promised number.</p><p><b>The annual full dress.</b> Once a year the drill scales to the continuity rehearsal: the restore run as if real — the runbook followed literally from its first line, the communications step actually drafted, the degraded-operation procedures walked at one branch (the paper fallback from the POS track's till procedures), and the re-entry of a simulated gap rehearsed from the paper trail. The full dress is scheduled, announced, and reviewed — its findings amend the runbook, which is the point.</p><p><b>The psychology, stated once.</b> Drills feel like waste precisely until they are survival, and the pressure to skip \"just this month\" arrives the first busy quarter. The counter is structural: the drill is a calendar line with an owner and an artifact, and a skipped drill is logged as skipped — visible in the twelve-line page. Two consecutive skips are an incident by policy.</p><p>Practical rule: the first drill ever run at a site is the most valuable single hour in this track — it converts every assumption on the continuity page into either evidence or a finding. Run it in week one of taking the role, not month one.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The before-change backup", "12", "<p>Backups defend against two event families: the catastrophic (rare) and the <b>self-inflicted</b> (regular). This chapter is the second family's discipline: the on-demand backup as the standing precondition for risky operations.</p><p><b>The rule.</b> Before any operation that changes data or structure at scale, a fresh on-demand backup is taken from the Download Backups page and its timestamp is written into the operation's log line — <i>backup 14:22 taken; import run 14:31.</i> The operations that qualify:</p><p>• Every <b>Data Import</b> beyond trivial size (module 3's step 4 — this chapter is where it comes from).<br>• Every <b>bulk edit</b> or bulk rename/merge batch.<br>• Every <b>customisation deployment</b> and settings change with data reach (module 5's change procedure includes this line).<br>• Every <b>update window</b> the host announces (the host backs up too; the site's own set is the belt to their braces).<br>• Anything the sysadmin hesitates over. Hesitation is the trigger; the backup costs one minute.</p><p><b>Why the timestamp matters.</b> The before-change backup's whole value is being <b>the last set before the event</b> — the exact restore point Chapter 4's surgical path needs. A recovery that has to choose between \"the nightly from before, losing the morning's trade\" and \"the nightly from after, containing the damage\" is a recovery the one-minute backup would have made trivial.</p><p><b>The paired habit — the operation log.</b> The backup timestamp lives in the ops log line that every governed operation already writes (imports: module 3; changes: module 5; incidents: module 7). The pairing means any operation can be unwound with precision: the log names what ran and when; the timestamp names the recovery point; the surgical path does the rest. An operation with no log line and no backup is the definition of ungoverned — whatever it was.</p><p><b>The recovery decision, when a change goes wrong.</b> The paths from Chapter 4, applied small:</p><p>1. <b>Reversible in-system?</b> The mis-imported records disabled or corrected by a counter-import; the bulk edit re-run with correct values. First choice — history stays whole.<br>2. <b>Surgical restore-to-read?</b> The before-change set to the test site; correct values read; production repaired forward. Second choice.<br>3. <b>Full restore to the before-change set?</b> Only when the damage is pervasive and the window since the backup is small — which, because the backup was taken <b>immediately</b> before, it usually is. This is the one scenario where full restore is routinely cheap: the window is minutes.</p><p>Practical rule: teach the rule as a reflex with one sentence — <b>no backup timestamp in the log line, no run.</b> It survives staff changes better than any paragraph.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The continuity runbook", "13", "<p>Backups recover data; <b>continuity</b> keeps the business operating while recovery happens. The runbook is one document, printed and stored where a dead site cannot take it, covering the four scenarios.</p><p><b>Scenario 1 — the site is down.</b></p><p>1. Confirm and scope (one user or all? the custody map's first question), note the exact time.<br>2. Raise with the host at incident severity — the five-item report; the runbook carries the support channel's details and the escalation path <b>on paper</b>.<br>3. Communicate inward: one message to staff from the sysadmin — what is known, what to do meanwhile, when the next update comes. Silence breeds improvisation; improvisation breeds the un-entered sale.<br>4. Degraded operation begins (scenario 4's procedures).<br>5. On restoration: the gap re-entered from the paper trail, the incident logged with timeline and cause.</p><p><b>Scenario 2 — data damage, site up.</b> The import/bulk-edit family: Chapter 4's three questions, the surgical path as default, the before-change set as the anchor, the repair forward, the log.</p><p><b>Scenario 3 — the security event.</b> A credential suspected compromised, a session that should not exist, an integration key exposed: the affected account disabled first and investigated second (disabling is reversible; the damage of waiting is not), passwords and keys rotated per module 8's procedures, the host informed where platform-side action is needed, and the event logged with its timeline. The runbook lists, on paper, who may order these steps.</p><p><b>Scenario 4 — degraded operation.</b> The business trades through the outage:</p><p>• <b>The tills:</b> the paper fallback from the POS track — pre-printed sale slips, the count discipline unchanged, everything re-entered in order on restoration. The runbook states where the pads live and who owns the re-entry.<br>• <b>Payments:</b> the release rules do not relax because the system is down — the mandate and the separation hold on paper; anything that cannot be verified waits.<br>• <b>The re-entry:</b> owned, sequenced (documents re-entered in occurrence order with honest dates), and reconciled — the first post-restoration close pays extra attention to the gap days.</p><p><b>The people layer.</b> The runbook's last page is names: the sysadmin, the backstop, the owner, the host's channel, the off-platform storage's location and access holders — with the standing rule that <b>the backstop can execute every scenario alone</b>, because incidents schedule themselves for leave days. Module 1's backstop design is load-bearing exactly here.</p><p><b>Keeping it live.</b> The runbook is amended by every drill's findings and every real incident's lessons, re-printed on amendment, and walked in the annual full dress. A runbook last touched two years ago is a museum piece describing a system that no longer exists.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's continuity year", "13", "<p>The survival layer, installed and tested. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The ground.</b> Module 1's audit line — <i>an untested backup</i> — unpacks into the standard findings: the host's nightly backups running faithfully (confirmed in writing: nightly, thirty-day retention, off-site replication; RPO twenty-four hours, RTO stated in hours); no site-side copy anywhere; no drill ever; no runbook; and the previous \"backup habit\" — an occasional database download by the old implementation partner — <b>database-only</b>, no files archives, ever.</p><p><b>Week one — the first drill.</b> Run per Chapter 5's advice in the first week of the role. The latest nightly set restores to the test site; the Trial Balance matches; the document counts agree — and check 3's attachment test <b>fails on the partner's old downloads</b>: the database-only habit is exposed on the spot as a restore that opens invoices whose evidence is gone. Finding logged; the completeness rule (database <b>plus both files archives</b>) written into the procedure; the drill signed at forty minutes end to end. The continuity page gets its first honest numbers.</p><p><b>Month one — the custodies established.</b> The off-platform copy procedure starts: full set downloaded after the drill, stored encrypted in business-controlled storage, access limited to the sysadmin and backstop, logged. The before-change rule is instituted with the reflex sentence — <i>no backup timestamp in the log line, no run</i> — and its first exercise arrives within weeks: module 3's price-list import runs with its 14:22 timestamp on record.</p><p><b>Month three — the rule pays for itself.</b> A supplier-record bulk edit misfires — a wrong field written across <b>136 suppliers</b>. The response is Chapter 6's decision tree run calmly: reversible in-system? No — the old values are gone from production. Surgical path: the before-change set (taken eleven minutes earlier) restores to the test site; the correct field values export; a governed Update-type import repairs production forward; total damage window, one hour of procurement queries; total data lost, <b>zero</b>. The incident log's closing line: <i>the one-minute backup bought back the afternoon.</i></p><p><b>Month six — the full dress.</b> The annual rehearsal runs early, announced: the runbook followed from line one for a simulated outage, the staff communication actually drafted and sent to a test list, the Ibadan tills trading ninety minutes on the paper pads with the count discipline holding, and the re-entry rehearsed in order the next morning. Findings: the runbook's paper copy was only at head office (branch copies printed), and one till had no pad (stocked). The runbook is amended, re-printed, and signed.</p><p><b>The year's page.</b> Twelve drill lines, zero skips. Off-platform copies: twelve, all verified. Before-change backups: every logged operation carries its timestamp. One real recovery, surgical, zero loss. The MD's note: <i>we used to hope; now we rehearse.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the fourth law", "11", "<p>The survival layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the untested backup.</b> Symptom: years of backup files, zero restores ever; confidence proportional to file count rather than evidence. Cause: presence mistaken for recoverability (Chapters 1, 5). Fix: the first drill this week, the monthly drill on the calendar with its signed line, and the skipped-drill-is-logged rule with two skips an incident.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the database-only habit.</b> Symptom: downloads and copies that carry the dump and never the files archives; a restore that opens invoices whose attachments are gone — the books stripped of their evidence. Cause: \"the backup\" misunderstood as one file (Chapter 2). Fix: the completeness rule — database plus both files archives, always, in both custodies — and the drill's attachment check enforcing it monthly.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the single custody.</b> Symptom: every backup living inside the platform it backs up; nothing the business itself holds. Cause: the host's competence mistaken for the whole design (Chapters 1, 3). Fix: the monthly off-platform copy — complete, encrypted, business-controlled, verified, logged — scheduled after the drill so it inherits proven restorability.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the assumed numbers.</b> Symptom: nobody can quote the RPO or RTO; everyone assumes better than the agreement states; the gap surfaces mid-incident. Cause: the recovery numbers never confirmed in writing (Chapter 2). Fix: both numbers on the continuity page, confirmed with the host, re-confirmed annually, and tested against the drill's timings.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — the unlogged operation.</b> Symptom: a damaged register and no before-change set; the recovery forced to choose between losing the morning and keeping the damage. Cause: the before-change rule absent or skipped under pressure (Chapter 6). Fix: the reflex sentence — no backup timestamp in the log line, no run — enforced by the ops-log pairing and audited in the monthly import-log reconciliation.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — the museum runbook.</b> Symptom: a continuity document nobody has opened in years, naming departed people and dead channels; the incident improvised around it. Cause: the runbook written once and never exercised (Chapter 7). Fix: amendment after every drill and incident, the annual full dress walking it literally, branch paper copies current, and the backstop able to execute every scenario alone.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Before the quiz, confirm you can: state the two custodies and what each covers; name a backup's two components and why files are part of the books; quote and source the RPO and RTO; run the daily presence check and the monthly off-platform copy from the Download Backups page with its custody rules; choose among the three restore paths with the three questions, and name sets by timestamp; run the monthly drill's five steps and the annual full dress; apply the before-change rule and its decision tree; and walk the runbook's four scenarios including degraded tills and governed re-entry.</p><p><b>The fourth law: a backup untested is a hope — copies in two custodies, complete to the file, restores rehearsed monthly, and continuity written where a dead site cannot take it.</b> Module 5 covers updates, changes, and the test discipline.</p>"),
]

